{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 proposes a 'shared mechanism' relation but identifies the mechanism as a structural analogy: both artifacts 'treat restatement as the failure mode and falsification as the success criterion.' This is a description of two artifacts sharing a high-level evaluative posture, not a demonstration that 084384ac0b1e's verdict is produced by the same causal mechanism as SRC_004's two-dimensional protocol. The target never states what the operative mechanism in 084384ac0b1e IS, only how its functions can be reorganized, so there is no shared causal mechanism—only a shared taxonomy of failure modes.","target":"418d1e834028b205e58079b94b1236a3d194daa54ba6900f0305385691493f0c"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 frames a 'depends on / contradicts' relation but the contradiction hinges on a causal claim: it asserts SRC_005's decorrelation criterion is unfalsifiable without case-level procedures because 'causal decorrelation' is unobservable in a single run. No causal mechanism is supplied for how a population-level covering principle would fail to bind individual runs; the target infers the principle is unfalsifiable from a measurement limitation rather than from a demonstrated incompatibility in the causal operation of SRC_005's criterion versus the case-level tests. Absent that mechanism, the relation reduces to asserting that SRC_005 is empirically under-determined—a property of evidence, not a substantive dependence/contradiction relation between artifacts.","target":"cb73ff0d078a6fa896841771c241a26a9804ca5897dd98795fb85a13217529fd"}]}